EXT. MONASTERY BELL TOWER - DAWN (1944)

A stone belfry open to the sky, half the roof gone. Below, the valley is smoke and orchard. A cracked bell hangs silent.

SERGEANT THADDEUS KRUGER, thirties, lies prone on the floor with a rifle. CORPORAL NIKOLAI BOGDANOV, twenties, crouches by the stair with a radio. A MONK, BROTHER ANSELMO, seventies, sits against the wall with rosary beads running through his fingers.

KRUGER
He's in the second window, third floor of the mill.

BOGDANOV
Who is?

KRUGER
The man who has been shooting at us since four.

BROTHER ANSELMO
He is a boy. I saw him yesterday, bringing water to the cows.

KRUGER
Then it's a boy with a rifle, Father.

BROTHER ANSELMO
I am not your father. I am only an old man who rings the bell.

BOGDANOV
Sir, command says hold until eight.

KRUGER
Command is thirty miles from here and has never smelled this smoke.

BOGDANOV
They've asked us to confirm positions.

KRUGER
Tell them we're in a bell tower on a hill, and we're not going anywhere.

BROTHER ANSELMO
Will you let me ring it?

KRUGER
No.

BROTHER ANSELMO
It would tell them we are here.

KRUGER
They know we are here.

BROTHER ANSELMO
It would tell them we are still alive.

Kruger lowers the rifle and looks at him. He studies him for a long moment. In the valley, a rooster crows.

KRUGER
Bogdanov.

BOGDANOV
Sergeant?

KRUGER
Cover the second window.

BOGDANOV
Yes, sir.

KRUGER
Father. One ring.

BROTHER ANSELMO
One ring.
